This research focuses on solving the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
\begin{split}
\left\{
\begin{array}{c}
\Delta^2u=\left(\frac{f(u)}{u^\alpha}\right)^{1/(2-\alpha)}\quad\text{in}\quad\mathbb{R}^4,\\
\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad u>0\quad\text{in}\quad\mathbb{R}^4,
\end{array}
\end{split}
\end{align*} where $\alpha\in (0,2)$ and $f$ satisfies certain conditions, particularly not being a polynomial. We demonstrate that the equation has a standardized solution in $H^2(\mathbb{R}^4)$ when the growth of $f$ is exponential, given as $\lim_{u\to \infty} \frac{f(u)}{e^{u^\beta}}=+\infty$ for some $\beta>1$.

